Give the softmax of the samplers a defined result for an infinite or a NaN logit
Fold-into: upstream/sampling

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- The shared softmax gives NaN probabilities for an infinite or a NaN logit.

  llama_sampler_softmax_impl computes exp(l - max_l). For l = max_l = +Inf or -Inf the value is
  NaN, and a NaN logit gives a NaN. The sum is then NaN. mirostat, mirostat v2 and adaptive-p draw
  from these probabilities with std::discrete_distribution, which asserts a positive sum
  (_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS) and has no defined result in a release build. A sorted array also takes
  max_l from the first candidate, which can be a NaN. The dist sampler has its own softmax, which
  patches/fuzz-core/0008 corrects in the same way.

  The change: max_l comes from a loop that a NaN never wins. When max_l is infinite, each candidate
  with the logit max_l gets an equal share of the probability. A NaN logit gets the probability 0.
  When each logit is NaN, each candidate gets an equal share. For finite logits, the probabilities
  are the same as those of the unpatched code.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_sampler--softmax-non-finite.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

Files:
    src/llama-sampler.cpp
--- a/src/llama-sampler.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-sampler.cpp
@@ -304,21 +304,43 @@ static void llama_sampler_softmax_impl(llama_token_data_array * cur_p, bool do_s
         llama_token_data_array_partial_sort_inplace(cur_p, cur_p->size);
     }
 
-    float max_l = cur_p->data[0].logit;
-    if (!cur_p->sorted) {
-        for (size_t i = 1; i < cur_p->size; ++i) {
-            max_l = std::max(max_l, cur_p->data[i].logit);
+    // The largest logit. A NaN logit never wins the compare, and the order of a sorted array with a
+    // NaN is not defined, thus the loop reads each logit.
+    float max_l = -INFINITY;
+    for (size_t i = 0; i < cur_p->size; ++i) {
+        if (cur_p->data[i].logit > max_l) {
+            max_l = cur_p->data[i].logit;
         }
     }
 
+    // exp(l - max_l) is NaN for l = max_l = +-Inf, and a NaN logit gives a NaN: the sum is then NaN,
+    // and a sampler that draws from the probabilities (std::discrete_distribution) has no valid
+    // weight. A +Inf candidate takes all the probability (an equal share for each one), all -Inf
+    // candidates share it, and a NaN logit gets none. For finite logits the values do not change.
     float cum_sum = 0.0f;
 
     for (size_t i = 0; i < cur_p->size; ++i) {
-        float p = expf(cur_p->data[i].logit - max_l);
+        const float l = cur_p->data[i].logit;
+        float p;
+        if (std::isnan(l)) {
+            p = 0.0f;
+        } else if (std::isinf(max_l)) {
+            p = l == max_l ? 1.0f : 0.0f;
+        } else {
+            p = expf(l - max_l);
+        }
         cur_p->data[i].p = p;
         cum_sum += p;
     }
 
+    // each logit is NaN: no candidate has information, thus each one gets an equal share
+    if (cum_sum == 0.0f) {
+        for (size_t i = 0; i < cur_p->size; ++i) {
+            cur_p->data[i].p = 1.0f;
+        }
+        cum_sum = (float) cur_p->size;
+    }
+
     for (size_t i = 0; i < cur_p->size; ++i) {
         cur_p->data[i].p /= cum_sum;
     }
